  "fields": {
    "title": "Cyberfeminism",
    "textContent": {
      "data": {},
      "content": [
        {
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": "Description",
              "nodeType": "text"
            }
          ],
          "nodeType": "heading-3"
        },
        {
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": "Cyberfeminism is a feminist approach that focuses on the relationship between cyberspace, the Internet, and technology. The term can refer to a philosophy, methodology, or community and is used to describe feminist practices that seek to theorize, critique, explore, and reshape the Internet, cyberspace, and new media technologies in general.",
              "nodeType": "text"
            }
          ],
          "nodeType": "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": "The term is strongly associated with western net art of the 1990s and changed over time. Today, the term technofeminism is more often used. Technofeminism increasingly deals with activist intersections of feminism and technology.",
              "nodeType": "text"
            },
            {
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "ydzXHO6QblPCWl9t0FElg",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "data": {},
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "value": "[1]",
                  "nodeType": "text"
                }
              ],
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink"
            },
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": "",
              "nodeType": "text"
            }
          ],
          "nodeType": "paragraph"
        },
        {
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": "Background",
              "nodeType": "text"
            }
          ],
          "nodeType": "heading-3"
        },
        {
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "data": {},
              "marks": [],
              "value": "The term cyberfeminism was coined in 1994 by Sadie Plant, director of the Cybernetic Culture Research Unit at the University of Warwick in the United Kingdom. The term is closely associated with Donna Haraway's cyborg manifesto, poststructuralist feminism, riot grrrl culture, and feminist critiques of the alleged erasure of women in discussions of technology. Although women have been actively involved in the development of new technologies throughout history, cyberfeminists have argued that technology is still seen as a male creation. Some of them argued that technology must always be questioned and reconceptualized.",
              "nodeType": "text"
            },
            {
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "ydzXHO6QblPCWl9t0FElg",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
